Object lock auth order leaks bucket lock configuration
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/security/findings/c74eddfc6a208191a95790611717414a?repo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fjustincormack%2Fargmin&sev=low
Criticality: low (attack path: low)
Status: fixed

# Resolution
Fixed in commit `a46b324` (`Align object lock auth order with AWS`).

We validated the AWS contract with a normal `s3-tests` integration test rather
than relying on inference from code inspection alone. The new test in
`crates/s3-tests/tests/object_lock.rs`
(`test_object_lock_unauthorized_calls_do_not_reveal_bucket_lock_configuration`)
compares unauthorized `Get/PutObjectRetention` and `Get/PutObjectLegalHold`
calls on a plain bucket versus an Object Lock bucket and passed against AWS.

That confirmed the report was valid: unauthorized callers must not be able to
distinguish the two bucket types. The fix in `a46b324` moves the
`ensure_object_lock_bucket` check in
`lock_object_for_authorized_object_lock` to after the authorization decision,
so unauthorized callers now receive the same `AccessDenied` response on both
bucket types. The change also added a matching `server-core` regression test.

