## Rubric
- [ ] Build debug binary and run targeted object-lock operation to observe runtime error difference (blocked: libisal missing).
- [ ] Attempt sanitizer/valgrind/debugger instrumentation (blocked: stable toolchain/no valgrind/gdb).
- [x] Confirm ensure_object_lock_bucket returns InvalidRequest when Object Lock is disabled (coordinator.rs:6623-6629).
- [x] Confirm lock_object_for_authorized_object_lock calls ensure before authorization and only later returns AccessDenied (coordinator.rs:6781-6809).
- [x] Verify object-lock APIs route through this helper and prior commit authorized before ensure (coordinator.rs:6945-7075; parent coordinator.rs:6633-6644).
## Report
Dynamic validation attempts failed due to missing dependencies/tooling. `cargo build -p argmin-s3` and `cargo build -p server-core` both abort in `crates/ec-sys` with `libisal not found; install intel-isa-l or set PKG_CONFIG_PATH` (build-script panic). An ASan attempt with `RUSTFLAGS='-Z sanitizer=address'` failed because the stable toolchain rejects `-Z` nightly flags. `valgrind` and `gdb` are not installed (`command not found`), so valgrind/debugger traces were not possible.

Code analysis confirms the info leak. In the current commit, `ensure_object_lock_bucket` returns `ServerError::InvalidRequest` with reason "Bucket is missing Object Lock Configuration" when Object Lock is disabled (crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:6623-6629). `lock_object_for_authorized_object_lock` now calls `ensure_object_lock_bucket` immediately after loading the bucket summary and expected owner check, before bucket-policy authorization; AccessDenied is only returned later after `requester_can_manage_object_lock_with_bucket_policy` (coordinator.rs:6781-6809). The object-lock API handlers (`get_object_retention_for_request`, `put_object_retention_for_request`, `get_object_legal_hold_for_request`, `put_object_legal_hold_for_request`) all route through this helper (coordinator.rs:6945-7075), so an unauthorized caller can distinguish buckets with Object Lock disabled by receiving `InvalidRequest` instead of `AccessDenied`.

The previous commit shows `lock_object_for_authorized_object_lock` authorizing first via `authorize_bucket_admin_requester` and only then calling `ensure_object_lock_bucket` (parent coordinator.rs:6633-6644), which would have masked this configuration detail with `AccessDenied`. This ordering change plausibly introduces the reported information disclosure.

